    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old female named Virginia with severe bilateral central vision loss due to dry age-related macular degeneration (visual acuity 20/200 bilaterally) is brought to the clinic by her family. The family is highly concerned because the patient reports seeing colorful, detailed images of ''cartoons, flowers, and children'' sitting in her living room. The patient is fully aware that these figures are not real and has no cognitive impairment, memory loss, or auditory hallucinations. What is the most likely diagnosis and underlying mechanism?', '["Charles Bonnet Syndrome; visual release hallucinations caused by sensory deprivation (loss of visual input) leading to spontaneous, hyperactive firing of cortical visual association areas", "Anticholinergic toxicity; acute delirium induced by over-the-counter sleep aids", "Dementia with Lewy Bodies; progressive cortical neurodegeneration characterized by visual hallucinations, parkinsonism, and fluctuating cognition", "Late-onset schizophrenia; chronic primary psychotic disorder characterized by complex visual hallucinations and persecutory delusions", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease; rapidly progressive prion-induced encephalopathy causing cortical blindness and hallucinations"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Charles Bonnet Syndrome (CBS)** is characterized by **complex, vivid visual hallucinations** in patients with **severe visual impairment** (most commonly macular degeneration, cataracts, or glaucoma). Pathophysiology involves **sensory deprivation (deafferentation)**: when visual input to the brain is lost, the visual association cortex is no longer inhibited and begins to fire spontaneously and hyperactively (visual release phenomenon). Crucially, **cognition is intact, and patients retain insight** — they know the hallucinations are not real. CBS is often misdiagnosed as dementia, schizophrenia, or delirium, causing unnecessary anxiety. Reassurance is the primary intervention. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 44; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
