    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old female named Lillian with severe bilateral central vision loss due to dry age-related macular degeneration (visual acuity 20/200 bilaterally) is brought to the clinic by her family. The family is highly concerned because the patient reports seeing colorful, detailed images of ''cartoons, flowers, and children'' sitting in her living room. The patient is fully aware that these figures are not real and has no cognitive impairment, memory loss, or auditory hallucinations. What is the most likely diagnosis and underlying mechanism?', '["Charles Bonnet Syndrome; visual release hallucinations caused by sensory deprivation (loss of visual input) leading to spontaneous, hyperactive firing of cortical visual association areas", "Anticholinergic toxicity; acute delirium induced by over-the-counter sleep aids", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease; rapidly progressive prion-induced encephalopathy causing cortical blindness and hallucinations", "Dementia with Lewy Bodies; progressive cortical neurodegeneration characterized by visual hallucinations, parkinsonism, and fluctuating cognition", "Late-onset schizophrenia; chronic primary psychotic disorder characterized by complex visual hallucinations and persecutory delusions"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Charles Bonnet Syndrome (CBS)** is characterized by **complex, vivid visual hallucinations** in patients with **severe visual impairment** (most commonly macular degeneration, cataracts, or glaucoma). Pathophysiology involves **sensory deprivation (deafferentation)**: when visual input to the brain is lost, the visual association cortex is no longer inhibited and begins to fire spontaneously and hyperactively (visual release phenomenon). Crucially, **cognition is intact, and patients retain insight** — they know the hallucinations are not real. CBS is often misdiagnosed as dementia, schizophrenia, or delirium, causing unnecessary anxiety. Reassurance is the primary intervention. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 44; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old male named Edward with long-standing, severe bilateral presbycusis and poor compliance with hearing aids presents with a complaint of hearing familiar melodies and hymns playing continuously in his head. The patient does not own a radio, has normal cognitive function, no history of psychiatric illness, and is fully aware that the music is an auditory illusion. What is the most likely diagnosis and underlying mechanism?', '["Herpes simplex virus encephalitis; localized temporal lobe infection causing complex auditory hallucinations and focal seizures", "Frontotemporal dementia; selective degeneration of the auditory cortex causing loss of semantic insight and musical hallucinations", "Schizophrenia; presenting with primary psychotic auditory verbal hallucinations and persecutory delusions", "Musical hallucinations (auditory release phenomenon); sensory deprivation of the auditory cortex due to severe hearing loss leads to spontaneous activation of central neural networks", "Salicylate overdose; acute toxicity causing musical hallucinations as a primary symptom with no change in hearing"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Similar to Charles Bonnet Syndrome in the visual system, **musical hallucinations (auditory release phenomenon)** occur in older adults with **severe, long-standing hearing loss**. Sensory deprivation leads to a loss of normal inhibitory input to the auditory association cortex, causing cortical hyperexcitability and spontaneous firing that is interpreted by the brain as complex auditory stimuli (often familiar melodies, songs, or hymns). The patient **retains insight** and knows no real music is playing. This is a benign condition related to hearing loss, not a psychiatric disorder or dementia. Treatment involves optimizing hearing aids and environmental sound enrichment. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; Oliver Sacks'' *Musicophilia*; Current Geriatrics)'),
