    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old male named Roy with presbyopia and mild cataracts has experienced two falls at home at night. As part of a home safety evaluation, which of the following lighting modifications is most appropriate to reduce his fall risk?', '["Install extremely bright, direct spotlight bulbs facing the stairs to maximize visual illumination, regardless of glare", "Install high-intensity, glare-free indirect LED lighting with nightlights along pathways, and use contrasting colored tape on the edges of stairs; avoid high-glare bulbs", "Recommend using a single handheld flashlight as the sole light source when walking at night to conserve home energy", "Keep the home in dim lighting at all times to allow the eyes to remain continuously dark-adapted", "Replace all white light bulbs with red light bulbs to enhance macular cone adaptation and pupillary response"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Visual changes in older adults require **lighting optimization** to prevent falls: (1) **Increase illumination** (older eyes need 3-4 times more light than younger eyes to see clearly), (2) **Minimize glare** (glare from exposed bulbs or polished floors scatters light in a cataractous lens, blinding the patient), (3) **Uniform illumination** (delayed dark/light adaptation makes moving from a bright room to a dark hallway hazardous), (4) **Nightlights along pathways** (bedroom to bathroom), and (5) **High-contrast markings** (edges of steps). Spotlights create harsh shadows and glare, which increase fall risk. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12; AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines; Current Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old female named Helen with presbyopia and mild cataracts has experienced two falls at home at night. As part of a home safety evaluation, which of the following lighting modifications is most appropriate to reduce her fall risk?', '["Replace all white light bulbs with red light bulbs to enhance macular cone adaptation and pupillary response", "Keep the home in dim lighting at all times to allow the eyes to remain continuously dark-adapted", "Recommend using a single handheld flashlight as the sole light source when walking at night to conserve home energy", "Install high-intensity, glare-free indirect LED lighting with nightlights along pathways, and use contrasting colored tape on the edges of stairs; avoid high-glare bulbs", "Install extremely bright, direct spotlight bulbs facing the stairs to maximize visual illumination, regardless of glare"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Visual changes in older adults require **lighting optimization** to prevent falls: (1) **Increase illumination** (older eyes need 3-4 times more light than younger eyes to see clearly), (2) **Minimize glare** (glare from exposed bulbs or polished floors scatters light in a cataractous lens, blinding the patient), (3) **Uniform illumination** (delayed dark/light adaptation makes moving from a bright room to a dark hallway hazardous), (4) **Nightlights along pathways** (bedroom to bathroom), and (5) **High-contrast markings** (edges of steps). Spotlights create harsh shadows and glare, which increase fall risk. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12; AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines; Current Geriatrics)'),
