    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old female named Jean with presbyopia and mild cataracts has experienced two falls at home at night. As part of a home safety evaluation, which of the following lighting modifications is most appropriate to reduce her fall risk?', '["Keep the home in dim lighting at all times to allow the eyes to remain continuously dark-adapted", "Replace all white light bulbs with red light bulbs to enhance macular cone adaptation and pupillary response", "Install high-intensity, glare-free indirect LED lighting with nightlights along pathways, and use contrasting colored tape on the edges of stairs; avoid high-glare bulbs", "Install extremely bright, direct spotlight bulbs facing the stairs to maximize visual illumination, regardless of glare", "Recommend using a single handheld flashlight as the sole light source when walking at night to conserve home energy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Visual changes in older adults require **lighting optimization** to prevent falls: (1) **Increase illumination** (older eyes need 3-4 times more light than younger eyes to see clearly), (2) **Minimize glare** (glare from exposed bulbs or polished floors scatters light in a cataractous lens, blinding the patient), (3) **Uniform illumination** (delayed dark/light adaptation makes moving from a bright room to a dark hallway hazardous), (4) **Nightlights along pathways** (bedroom to bathroom), and (5) **High-contrast markings** (edges of steps). Spotlights create harsh shadows and glare, which increase fall risk. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12; AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines; Current Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old male named James is noted to have a significant decline in his ability to identify common odors during a clinical examination. What is the primary underlying anatomical and physiological change responsible for age-related olfactory loss (presbyosmia)?', '["Selective atrophy of the trigeminal nerve (CN V) general sensory pathways in the nasal cavity", "Deposition of calcium pyrophosphate crystals within the nasal septum and cribriform plate", "Hypersecretion of serous glands within the Bowman''s capsules of the olfactory epithelium", "Degeneration and reduction of olfactory receptor neurons in the nasal mucosa, thinning of the cribriform plate, and loss of glomeruli volume within the olfactory bulb", "Severe hypertrophy of the turbinates with permanent mechanical obstruction of the olfactory cleft"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Age-related olfactory decline (**presbyosmia**) affects up to 75% of individuals over age 80. The primary pathophysiology involves: (1) **Degeneration and decreased replacement of olfactory receptor neurons** in the olfactory neuroepithelium, (2) **Thinning of the cribriform plate**, and (3) **Loss of olfactory bulb volume and glomeruli** (due to decreased sensory input and age-related neurodegeneration). Olfactory decline is also a well-recognized early biomarker for neurodegenerative diseases like Parkinson''s disease and Alzheimer''s disease. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 46; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12)'),
