    (v_topic_id, 'A 97-year-old female named Eleanor presents with involuntary weight loss of 12 lbs over the past 3 months. Her medical workup for malignancy and depression is completely negative. She reports that ''food just doesn''t taste good anymore'' and her appetite is severely diminished. Further testing reveals profound hyposmia. Which of the following best explains this clinical presentation?', '["Taste buds on the tongue are completely degenerated by age 70, preventing the perception of basic sweet and salty tastes", "The patient has a central chewing apraxia caused by sensory deafferentation of the trigeminal nerve", "Olfactory loss causes a reflexive hypersecretion of gastric acid, leading to early satiety and chronic dyspepsia", "Anosmia causes chronic malabsorption of micronutrients and fats in the duodenum", "Flavor perception is primarily driven by retronasal olfaction; age-related olfactory loss impairs the ability to enjoy food, leading to decreased appetite, poor caloric intake, and nutritional depletion"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Retronasal olfaction** (odor molecules passing from the oral cavity to the nasal cavity during chewing) is responsible for **up to 80-90% of flavor perception**. In contrast, taste buds only detect five basic tastes (sweet, salty, sour, bitter, umami). When an older adult experiences **olfactory decline**, they lose the ability to perceive flavor, making food bland and unappealing. This leads to **anorexia of aging, decreased caloric intake, involuntary weight loss, and nutritional deficiencies**. It is a major cause of functional decline in older adults. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 46; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-year-old male named David presents with involuntary weight loss of 15 lbs over the past 3 months. His medical workup for malignancy and depression is completely negative. He reports that ''food just doesn''t taste good anymore'' and his appetite is severely diminished. Further testing reveals profound hyposmia. Which of the following best explains this clinical presentation?', '["Olfactory loss causes a reflexive hypersecretion of gastric acid, leading to early satiety and chronic dyspepsia", "Anosmia causes chronic malabsorption of micronutrients and fats in the duodenum", "Flavor perception is primarily driven by retronasal olfaction; age-related olfactory loss impairs the ability to enjoy food, leading to decreased appetite, poor caloric intake, and nutritional depletion", "Taste buds on the tongue are completely degenerated by age 70, preventing the perception of basic sweet and salty tastes", "The patient has a central chewing apraxia caused by sensory deafferentation of the trigeminal nerve"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Retronasal olfaction** (odor molecules passing from the oral cavity to the nasal cavity during chewing) is responsible for **up to 80-90% of flavor perception**. In contrast, taste buds only detect five basic tastes (sweet, salty, sour, bitter, umami). When an older adult experiences **olfactory decline**, they lose the ability to perceive flavor, making food bland and unappealing. This leads to **anorexia of aging, decreased caloric intake, involuntary weight loss, and nutritional deficiencies**. It is a major cause of functional decline in older adults. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 46; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
