    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-year-old female named Dorothy presents with involuntary weight loss of 20 lbs over the past 5 months. Her medical workup for malignancy and depression is completely negative. She reports that ''food just doesn''t taste good anymore'' and her appetite is severely diminished. Further testing reveals profound hyposmia. Which of the following best explains this clinical presentation?', '["Anosmia causes chronic malabsorption of micronutrients and fats in the duodenum", "The patient has a central chewing apraxia caused by sensory deafferentation of the trigeminal nerve", "Taste buds on the tongue are completely degenerated by age 70, preventing the perception of basic sweet and salty tastes", "Flavor perception is primarily driven by retronasal olfaction; age-related olfactory loss impairs the ability to enjoy food, leading to decreased appetite, poor caloric intake, and nutritional depletion", "Olfactory loss causes a reflexive hypersecretion of gastric acid, leading to early satiety and chronic dyspepsia"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Retronasal olfaction** (odor molecules passing from the oral cavity to the nasal cavity during chewing) is responsible for **up to 80-90% of flavor perception**. In contrast, taste buds only detect five basic tastes (sweet, salty, sour, bitter, umami). When an older adult experiences **olfactory decline**, they lose the ability to perceive flavor, making food bland and unappealing. This leads to **anorexia of aging, decreased caloric intake, involuntary weight loss, and nutritional deficiencies**. It is a major cause of functional decline in older adults. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 46; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-year-old male named Lawrence with a history of long-standing hypertension and type 2 diabetes presents with progressively worsening blood pressure control (BP 156/95 mmHg) and elevated HbA1c (8.7%). A dietary review reveals that the patient has been heavily salting his meals and consuming highly sweetened foods. The patient explains that he does this because his food has become ''tasteless.'' What is the most likely underlying cause of this clinical change?', '["The patient has developed an underlying obsessive-compulsive disorder targeting sodium and glucose intake", "Age-related hypergeusia has hyper-sensitized the tongue''s sweet receptors, leading to sugar cravings", "The patient has developed a primary salt-secreting salivary gland tumor that alters the oral microbiome", "The patient has age-related hyposmia; the loss of flavor perception leads to a compensatory overreliance on basic tastes (sodium and sugar) to make food palatable, worsening his hypertension and glycemic control", "Diabetic nephropathy has altered the renal excretion of sodium, causing a systemic craving for salty foods"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Older adults with **olfactory decline (hyposmia)** often complain that food is bland. Because basic taste thresholds (sweet, salty) are relatively preserved compared to olfaction, these patients frequently **compensate by adding excessive amounts of salt and sugar** to their food to achieve some palatability. In patients with pre-existing comorbidities like **hypertension, congestive heart failure, or diabetes**, this compensatory dietary change can lead to **worsening blood pressure control, fluid overload, and poor glycemic control**. Clinicians must screen for olfactory loss and recommend safe flavor enhancers (spices, herbs, lemon juice, vinegar) instead of salt and sugar. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 46; Current Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
