    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old male named Henry with severe hyposmia lives independently. During a home safety evaluation, which of the following is the most critical recommendation to address his olfactory deficit and prevent life-threatening domestic hazards?', '["Recommend checking for gas leaks manually by applying soapy water to all gas pipes weekly", "No interventions are needed because utility companies add mercaptan to gas, which stimulates the trigeminal nerve independently of olfaction", "Install specialized natural gas and propane detectors in the kitchen, place visual strobe-light and audible smoke alarms in every room, and transition from a gas stove to an electric or induction cooktop", "Move the patient to a skilled nursing facility immediately, as anosmic patients cannot safely live independently under any circumstances", "Advise the patient to leave windows open continuously to ensure proper ventilation, regardless of outdoor temperature"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Olfactory decline represents a severe **safety hazard** for older adults living independently. They are **unable to smell gas leaks** (mercaptan is added to natural gas, but detection thresholds decline significantly with age), **smoke/fire, or spoiled food**. Home safety interventions are vital: (1) **Natural gas/propane detectors** with loud audible alarms, (2) **Electric/induction stoves** (eliminating the risk of an unlit gas burner leaking gas), and (3) **Functional smoke alarms**. Relying on trigeminal stimulation is unsafe because detection thresholds are highly variable and often elevated in older adults. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 46; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old male named Charles with severe hyposmia lives independently. During a home safety evaluation, which of the following is the most critical recommendation to address his olfactory deficit and prevent life-threatening domestic hazards?', '["Move the patient to a skilled nursing facility immediately, as anosmic patients cannot safely live independently under any circumstances", "Advise the patient to leave windows open continuously to ensure proper ventilation, regardless of outdoor temperature", "Recommend checking for gas leaks manually by applying soapy water to all gas pipes weekly", "Install specialized natural gas and propane detectors in the kitchen, place visual strobe-light and audible smoke alarms in every room, and transition from a gas stove to an electric or induction cooktop", "No interventions are needed because utility companies add mercaptan to gas, which stimulates the trigeminal nerve independently of olfaction"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Olfactory decline represents a severe **safety hazard** for older adults living independently. They are **unable to smell gas leaks** (mercaptan is added to natural gas, but detection thresholds decline significantly with age), **smoke/fire, or spoiled food**. Home safety interventions are vital: (1) **Natural gas/propane detectors** with loud audible alarms, (2) **Electric/induction stoves** (eliminating the risk of an unlit gas burner leaking gas), and (3) **Functional smoke alarms**. Relying on trigeminal stimulation is unsafe because detection thresholds are highly variable and often elevated in older adults. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 46; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12)'),
