    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old male named James with severe hyposmia lives independently. During a home safety evaluation, which of the following is the most critical recommendation to address his olfactory deficit and prevent life-threatening domestic hazards?', '["Advise the patient to leave windows open continuously to ensure proper ventilation, regardless of outdoor temperature", "Recommend checking for gas leaks manually by applying soapy water to all gas pipes weekly", "No interventions are needed because utility companies add mercaptan to gas, which stimulates the trigeminal nerve independently of olfaction", "Move the patient to a skilled nursing facility immediately, as anosmic patients cannot safely live independently under any circumstances", "Install specialized natural gas and propane detectors in the kitchen, place visual strobe-light and audible smoke alarms in every room, and transition from a gas stove to an electric or induction cooktop"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Olfactory decline represents a severe **safety hazard** for older adults living independently. They are **unable to smell gas leaks** (mercaptan is added to natural gas, but detection thresholds decline significantly with age), **smoke/fire, or spoiled food**. Home safety interventions are vital: (1) **Natural gas/propane detectors** with loud audible alarms, (2) **Electric/induction stoves** (eliminating the risk of an unlit gas burner leaking gas), and (3) **Functional smoke alarms**. Relying on trigeminal stimulation is unsafe because detection thresholds are highly variable and often elevated in older adults. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 46; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 97-year-old female named Louise with known anosmia is evaluated in the emergency department for severe gastroenteritis due to a foodborne bacterial infection. Her family notes that she frequently eats expired foods from the refrigerator. Which of the following is the most appropriate preventive recommendation for this patient?', '["Implement a system of clear date-labeling on all refrigerator items, have a family member or caregiver check the refrigerator weekly to discard expired food, and advise the patient to strictly follow expiration dates rather than relying on ''smell or taste''", "Recommend boiling all food (including fresh produce and dairy) for at least 30 minutes before consumption", "Prescribe daily prophylactic oral antibiotics to prevent future gastrointestinal infections", "Advise the patient that foodborne pathogens are killed by standard refrigeration, so expired food remains completely safe", "Perform an urgent upper endoscopy to evaluate for underlying age-related gastric hypoacidity and mucosal atrophy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Older adults with **anosmia (loss of smell)** are at high risk for **foodborne illnesses** because they cannot smell the sour or putrid odors of spoiled food (e.g., spoiled milk, rotten meat). This is compounded by age-related declines in gastric acid secretion (achlorhydria/hypochlorhydria) and immune senescence, making infections more severe. Prevention requires **active caregiver/family involvement** in date-labeling and regular refrigerator sweeps, and educating the patient to never eat food past its expiration date simply because ''it smells fine.'' (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 46; Current Geriatrics)'),
